WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.030
- [Aesha] When I do my work, I want it

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.610
to have this real visceral reaction

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:06.970
whether it's bringing them to tears,

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.403
whether it's inspiring them.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.230
I want to show the world
that beauty and grace

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.183
are not defined by status or race.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.360
My name is Aesha Ash, and through

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.560
the use of ballet, I expose my community

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.803
to more positive images of women of color.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:37.435
- [Yvonne] That's crazy.

00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:38.970
- [Aesha] That's where it all began.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.773
- [Yvonne] Wow, so this is it.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.900
Aesha grew up in the inner city

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.680
of Rochester, New York, where crime,

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.110
gun violence and poverty were amongst

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.520
the biggest threats to the community.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.100
- [Aesha] For us, that's normal.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.940
That's just life in the hood,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.360
but what I remember the
most is the dance studio.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.620
I started dancing all
together at the age of five.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.730
A teacher mentioned to my mother

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.110
that I had some promise in ballet,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.490
and that it would be difficult for me,

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:08.290
as a woman of color, to
enter the ballet world.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.491
- [Yvonne] But the adversity she faced

00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:11.324
was the very thing that pushed her

00:01:11.324 --> 00:01:13.220
to pursue a career in ballet.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.000
She went on to become one of the few

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.700
women of color to ever grace the stages

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.460
of New York City Ballet
School of American Ballet.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.560
- [Aesha] I felt like my
entire career was catching up,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.610
and then, being a black
woman in the ballet world,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.750
I felt like I had to work twice as hard

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.250
because of that as well;
and there's so much

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.750
negative stereotyping
and misrepresentations

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.520
of who we are perpetuated in the media.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.103
So, I began the Swan
Dreams Project in 2011.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.080
Through the use of imagery and my career

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.330
as a ballet dancer, I
challenge stereotypes

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:48.850
that exist for women of color,

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.433
particularly those from
inner-city communities.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.586
I didn't see anyone that had left

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.360
the inner city of Rochester, New York,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.350
and come back here, and sort of

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.220
expose all of use to
look what you can become,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.963
look what I went off to do.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.670
- [Yvonne] Aesha put on her tutu,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.060
grabbed a photographer, and started making

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.570
her way through the very
streets where she grew up.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.560
- [Aesha] And it was very profound

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.910
the impact that I got
back, especially from

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.957
the people on the street.

00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:27.090
Thank you, thank you for that.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.620
There was the one image that went viral

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.840
of me with the little girls,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.420
and I just pulled them to the side,

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.610
just started doing some ballet class.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.330
And that's when I
realized I had something.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.427
This has to be bigger than this.

00:02:37.427 --> 00:02:39.810
And it began morphing and changing,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.880
and then people began
asking for appearances.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:43.713
Good job!

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:45.743
Who's next?

00:02:45.743 --> 00:02:46.771
Let me see you.

00:02:46.771 --> 00:02:48.800
It's important that
our girls see that side

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.783
of themselves because for many kids

00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:54.820
it's hard to be what you can't see.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.850
I didn't see a black ballerina before

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.350
I decided that's what I wanted to be.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.730
It was really important for me

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.630
to be in my environment displaying that

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.180
because this is where I'm from.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.113
It was always beyond ballet.

